For an ideal inductor, \[v(t)=L\frac{di(t)}{dt}\] A constant direct current can be represented as a step current: \[i(t)=I\,u(t)\] where \(u(t)\) is the unit-step function. Differentiating, \[\frac{di(t)}{dt}
=
I\frac{du(t)}{dt}\] Using \[\frac{du(t)}{dt}=\delta(t)\] we get \[\frac{di(t)}{dt}
=
I\delta(t)\] Hence, \[v(t)
=
L\frac{di(t)}{dt}
=
LI\delta(t)\] Since \(\delta(t)\) is an impulse function, the applied voltage must be an ideal impulse. Therefore, \[\boxed{\textbf{C. }\text{Ideal impulse}}\]

Given, \[L=2~\text{H},\qquad
R_1=10~\Omega,\qquad
R_2=2~\Omega,\qquad
C=0.25~\mu\text{F}\] <img src="img/03196e520c605f7f.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,333px)"> <strong>(a) Determine \(i_L(0)\) and \(V_C(0)\)</strong><br/>Before switching, the circuit has been connected to the \(12\) V source for a long time. At steady state, \[\text{Inductor} \rightarrow \text{Short Circuit}, \qquad \text{Capacitor} \rightarrow \text{Open Circuit}\] Therefore, the equivalent circuit is <img src="img/a98d3e5c66cba0d4.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,275px)"> \[I=\frac{12}{R_1+R_2}
=\frac{12}{10+2}
=1~\text{A}\] Hence, \[\boxed{i_L(0^-)=1~\text{A}}\] Since the current through an inductor cannot change instantaneously, \[\boxed{i_L(0^+)=i_L(0^-)=1~\text{A}}\] The capacitor voltage is the voltage across \(R_2\), \[V_C(0^-)=R_2I
=2\times1
=2~\text{V}\] Since the voltage across a capacitor cannot change instantaneously, \[\boxed{V_C(0^+)=V_C(0^-)=2~\text{V}}\] <strong>(b) Differential equation governing \(V_C(t)\) for \(t&gt;0\)</strong><br/><img src="img/f320188405f8c2a9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,333px)"> For \(t&gt;0\), the switch is connected to the \(24\) V source. The capacitor current is \[i_C=C\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}\] The current through \(R_2\) is \[i_{R_2}=\frac{V_C(t)}{R_2}
=\frac{V_C(t)}{2}\] Applying KCL at the capacitor node, \[i_L=i_C+i_{R_2}\] \[i_L
=
C\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
+\frac{V_C(t)}{2}
\tag{1}\] Applying KVL, \[L\frac{di_L}{dt}+R_1i_L+V_C(t)-24=0\] Substituting \(L=2\) H and \(R_1=10~\Omega\), \[2\frac{di_L}{dt}+10i_L+V_C(t)-24=0
\tag{2}\] Differentiating (1), \[\frac{di_L}{dt}
=
C\frac{d^2V_C(t)}{dt^2}
+\frac{1}{2}\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}\] Substituting \(i_L\) and \(di_L/dt\) into (2), \[2\left(
C\frac{d^2V_C(t)}{dt^2}
+\frac{1}{2}\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
\right)
+
10\left(
C\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
+\frac{V_C(t)}{2}
\right)
+
V_C(t)
=
24\] \[2C\frac{d^2V_C(t)}{dt^2}
+
(1+10C)\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
+
6V_C(t)
=
24\] Substituting \[C=0.25\times10^{-6}\ \text{F}\] \[0.5\times10^{-6}\frac{d^2V_C(t)}{dt^2}
+
(1+2.5\times10^{-6})\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
+
6V_C(t)
=
24\] Multiplying throughout by \(2\times10^6\), \[\boxed{
\frac{d^2V_C(t)}{dt^2}
+
2\times10^6\frac{dV_C(t)}{dt}
+
12\times10^6V_C(t)
=
48\times10^6
}\] <strong>(c) Steady-state value of \(V_C(t)\)</strong><br/><img src="img/da009a77ea995f69.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,275px)"> \[I
=
\frac{24}{10+2}
=
2~\text{A}\] The capacitor voltage is equal to the voltage across \(R_2\), \[V_C(\infty)
=
R_2I
=
2\times2
=
4~\text{V}\] Therefore, \[\boxed{V_C(\infty)=4~\text{V}}\]

questions.question:
The state-space representation of a system is given by: \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-5 &amp; 1\\
-6 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}\] Find the Laplace transform of the state transition matrix. Find also the value of \(x_1\) at \(t=1\) if \(x_1(0)=1\) and \(x_2(0)=0\).

questions.explanation:
Given, \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-5 &amp; 1\\
-6 &amp; 0
\end{bmatrix}\] The Laplace transform of the state transition matrix is \[\Phi(s)
=
(sI-A)^{-1}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
s+5 &amp; -1\\
6 &amp; s
\end{bmatrix}^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s(s+5)+6}
\begin{bmatrix}
s &amp; 1\\
-6 &amp; s+5
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\begin{bmatrix}
s &amp; 1\\
-6 &amp; s+5
\end{bmatrix}\] Since \[x(t)=e^{At}x(0)
=
\mathcal{L}^{-1}\{\Phi(s)\}x(0)\] Given, \[x(0)=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\
0
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x_1(t)
=
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{s}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\right\}\] \[x_1(t)
=
-2e^{-2t}
+
3e^{-3t}\] At \(t=1\), \[x_1(1)
=
-2e^{-2}
+
3e^{-3}
=-0.121\] \[\boxed{
\Phi(s)
=
\frac{1}{(s+2)(s+3)}
\begin{bmatrix}
s &amp; 1\\
-6 &amp; s+5
\end{bmatrix}
}\] \[\boxed{
x_1(1)=
-0.121
}\]

