Hysteresis loss and eddy current loss relations are: \[P_{h}\propto f(B_{m})^{2}, \qquad P_{e}\propto V^{2}\] Given, \[V_{2}=0.9V_{1}, \qquad f_{2}=45\,\text{Hz}=0.9f_{1}\] Since, \[\frac{V_{2}}{f_{2}}=\frac{V_{1}}{f_{1}}\] flux density remains constant. Hence, \[P_{h}\propto f\] \[P_{h2}=0.9P_{h1}\] Also, \[P_{e2}=(0.9)^{2}P_{e1}=0.81P_{e1}\] Initially, \[P_{h1}=P_{e1}=\frac{P_{\text{core1}}}{2}\] Therefore, \[P_{\text{core2}}
=
0.9\left(\frac{P_{\text{core1}}}{2}\right)
+
0.81\left(\frac{P_{\text{core1}}}{2}\right)\] \[P_{\text{core2}}
=
0.855P_{\text{core1}}\] \[\%\text{ change}
=
\frac{0.855P_{\text{core1}}-P_{\text{core1}}}
{P_{\text{core1}}}\times100
=
-14.5\%\] Therefore, core loss reduces by \(14.5\%\). \[\boxed{C}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Hysteresis loss: \(P_{h}\propto f(B_{m})^{2}\)</li><li>Eddy current loss: \(P_{e}\propto V^{2}\)</li><li>Constant \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) implies constant flux density.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not directly scale hysteresis loss with voltage.</li><li>Check whether \(\dfrac{V}{f}\) remains constant before changing \(B_{m}\).</li></ul></div>

