A synchronous generator connected to an infinite bus and operating under over-excited condition supplies reactive power to the system. For a synchronous generator, \[Q=\frac{EV}{X_s}\cos\delta-\frac{V^2}{X_s}\] When excitation is increased, \[E &gt; V\] and the generator delivers positive reactive power (\(Q&gt;0\)) to the bus. A source supplying reactive power behaves as a capacitor from the system point of view. \[\text{Over-excited Generator}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
Q&gt;0
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\text{Capacitive Behaviour}\] Hence, the machine acts as a capacitor. \[\boxed{\text{A}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>An over-excited synchronous machine supplies reactive power to the system.</li><li>A source supplying reactive power is equivalent to a capacitive element.</li><li>An under-excited synchronous machine absorbs reactive power and behaves like an inductor.</li><li>Infinite bus maintains constant voltage and frequency irrespective of generator excitation changes.</li></ul></div>

